# Carrier Wound #30: The Agent Dictated How Many Bytes Hit The Disk

Wound #29 bounded the debug transcript and added the `diag()` channel that
survives an abort. We shipped it, deployed it, and re-drove a dream-shape run on
the fixed code. The lane advanced PAST `build-transcript` (`write_exit: 0`,
exactly the #29 receipt's called shot) and died one step later at
`build-receipt` — and this time the diagnostics channel rode out on the operator
blocker carrying the numbers, no staring:

```
run-live-20260614T183519765Z-53cc30a4  →  blocked
Agent lane reached step "build-receipt"
[lane diagnostics:
  build-transcript disk: 30168 KB free of 1900000-ish KB ...
  build-transcript raw_output_bytes: 1084434255            ← 1.08 GB
  build-transcript lane_output_bytes: 112                  ← ENOSPC stub
  build-transcript write_exit: 0
  ...
  cp: error writing 'run/planner-blueprint.json': No space left on device ]
```

The #29 receipt's "What this does NOT claim" predicted this verbatim: *"If the
next honest run shows the lane now advances to `build-receipt` and dies THERE,
that is the next wound."* The diagnostics named the true culprit, and it was not
the transcript at all — it was **the raw pi output itself: 1.08 GB on a 1.81 GB
`lite` disk**. The transcript bound (#29) worked perfectly and was never the real
hog; #29 was defensive-under-every-hypothesis on purpose, which is why it
advanced the ball even though my root-cause guess (4× transcript double-write)
was wrong. The diagnostics corrected the guess. That is the whole point of the
channel.

## Mechanism — the lane let the agent decide how many bytes to write

`pi`'s stdout is the SOURCE bucket every later step draws from: `normalize-output`
copies it to `LANE_OUTPUT_PATH`, `build-transcript` samples it, `build-receipt`
reads + hashes + `cp`s it. The capture was a bare redirect with no ceiling:

```bash
timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi ... -p "$(cat "$LANE_PROMPT_PATH")" > "$raw_output_path" 2> "$stderr_path"
```

A runaway/looping planner — amplified under `--mode json`, where every token and
tool call is a stream event — produced a **1.08 GB** raw file. (It also blew its
546s budget and was timeout-killed, exit 124; whether the spew caused the loop or
the loop caused the spew is the deeper question below.) On the `lite` disk that
left ~30 MB free, and then every downstream consumer inherited the dead disk:
`normalize-output` timed out trying to parse 1 GB → `persist_unnormalized_output`'s
`cp` ENOSPC'd to a 112-byte stub (`lane_output_bytes: 112` is the fingerprint) →
`build-transcript` (capped by #29) squeaked through in the last 30 MB →
`build-receipt`'s `cp run/planner-blueprint.json` hit ENOSPC and aborted exit 143.
546s burned, nothing committed.

> In the abstract, an unbounded result set occurs when the caller allows the
> other system to dictate terms. It is a failure in handshaking. In any API or
> protocol, the caller should always indicate how much of a response it is
> prepared to accept. […] beware any relationship that can accumulate unbounded
> results.
>
> — Michael Nygard, *Release It!*, §4.2 Unbounded Result Sets

That is this wound exactly. The lane was the caller; `pi` was the other system;
the lane indicated NO limit on how much it would accept, so a misbehaving agent
dictated the terms — and the terms were 1 GB. Wound #29 drained the transcript
bucket one consumer deep; this is the SAME pattern one bucket deeper, at the
source that fills all of them. Bound the source and every downstream bucket
drains with it.

## Fix — the caller states how much it will accept, at the source

`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`, the `pi-invoke` block. Cap what
reaches disk with `head -c`, which is the lane's half of the handshake:

```bash
raw_output_cap_bytes=67108864            # 64 MiB; test-overridable via PIWF_RAW_OUTPUT_CAP_BYTES
timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi ... -p "$(cat "$LANE_PROMPT_PATH")" 2> "$stderr_path" \
  | head -c "$raw_output_cap_bytes" > "$raw_output_path"
pi_status=$PIPESTATUS
raw_output_capture_bytes="$(wc -c < "$raw_output_path" ...)"
if [ "$raw_output_capture_bytes" -ge "$raw_output_cap_bytes" ]; then
  printf '\n[pi-invoke output cap] pi raw output reached the %s-byte capture cap ...' ...
  diag "pi-invoke raw_output_capped: reached $raw_output_cap_bytes byte cap ..."
fi
```

Three properties, all load-bearing:

1. **Disk can never overflow.** `head -c` writes at most the cap to the file no
   matter what pi emits. A 1 GB (or 1 TB) spew lands as 64 MiB on disk; every
   downstream `cp`/hash/`git add` now runs against a healthy disk. The cascade is
   cut at the root — one bound drains all four buckets.
2. **A runaway is bounded in wall-clock too, not just disk.** `head` closes the
   pipe at the cap, so pi takes SIGPIPE on its next write and stops. The runaway
   no longer rides its full timeout budget; it dies when it overflows the cap.
3. **Honest, named, diagnosable failure instead of a blind ENOSPC cascade.** When
   the cap is hit, the truncation is announced in pi's stderr tail (which the
   receipt carries) AND in the `diag()` channel (which rides out on the operator
   blocker). The committed receipt is `status:"failed"` with the truncated head —
   not a 143 abort with nothing committed. Runaway detection keys on FILE SIZE
   (`>= cap`), not on `pi_status`, so it is correct whether pi exits 0 (fully
   buffered then truncated), 141 (SIGPIPE on cap), or 124 (timeout).

The cap is generous — 64 MiB is orders of magnitude over any real planner
blueprint or event stream (the live runaway was 1.08 GB, ~16,000× the cap), so
only the pathological case is ever truncated. A legitimate run is untouched.

### String.raw note (the convention bit twice this time — record it harder)

`buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` is a `String.raw` template, so JS `${...}`
interpolation fires ANYWHERE in the template — including inside bash comments. The
first cut wrote `pi_status=${PIPESTATUS[0]}` and threw `ReferenceError:
PIPESTATUS is not defined` at build time. The fix is **bare `$PIPESTATUS`**, which
in bash expands to `${PIPESTATUS[0]}` (an array referenced with no subscript is
element 0) — same value, no brace, no collision. Then the EXPLANATORY COMMENT I
added still contained a literal `${PIPESTATUS[0]}` and threw the SAME error from
inside a comment. Lesson, now triple-paid (this wound + #29's two PARSE_ERRORs):
**no `${...}` of any kind in this template's bash, not in code, not in comments.**
Bare `$var` only; defaults under `set +u`/`set -u`.

## Chaos test that would have caught it (hostile double, not a polite mock)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` —
*"bounds the raw pi-output capture at the source so a runaway stream cannot
ENOSPC the lane (wound #30)"*. It SLICES the REAL pi-invoke capture block out of
`buildPiAgentLaneCommand()`'s emitted bash (`command.indexOf('pi_mode_args=""')`
→ `command.indexOf("\nmark normalize-output")`, throwing if either anchor moves)
and runs it verbatim under a faithful harness (real `diag()`, real budget
arithmetic, real `timeout`/`head` pipe) against a `pi` that **spews 512 KiB**
into a **4096-byte** cap. Asserts: the raw capture file is bounded to exactly the
cap (`rawBytes === 4096` — a 1 GB spew can't reach disk), the stderr carries the
`[pi-invoke output cap]` notice, the diagnostics record `pi-invoke
raw_output_capped`, and the script reaches a `POST_PI_REACHED` sentinel instead
of dying in an ENOSPC cascade.

**Pre-fix:** the bare `> "$raw_output_path"` writes the FULL 512 KiB (1 GB in
prod), with no notice and no diag → `rawBytes` is 524288 not 4096, and every
signal flips false. This is a hostile double in the franchise sense
([[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]): it reproduces the production transport — the
real emitted bash, a real oversized stream through a real `head -c` pipe — rather
than a polite mock that emits a few tidy bytes and never overflows anything.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · `pnpm vitest run` =
**55 files / 427 passed** (incl. the new chaos test). The pre-existing pi-invoke
hung-test (`pi_status=124`) stays green: an empty capture (`0 -ge cap` is false)
skips the runaway branch and the SIGPIPE/timeout semantics are unchanged.

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held — and this is the load-bearing part)

**The carrier bound makes failure honest and disk-safe. It does NOT, by itself,
make a dream run reach `captured`.** If the planner pi genuinely loops and never
emits a valid blueprint, capping its output converts an ENOSPC cascade into a
clean `planner_output_invalid`/runaway receipt — better, observable, committed —
but still not a capture. The actual path to `captured` (Joel's stated goal: *"a
dream shaped run actually reaches captured"*) needs the DEEPER answer: **why does
the planner pi produce 1 GB and blow its 546s budget?** Three live runs in a row
now exhibit it (a8bc84dc, f9a37a09, 53cc30a4). The bounded re-drive is the probe:

- If the lane now reaches `captured`, the 1 GB was stream bloat that starved
  `normalize-output`; capping the source freed the disk AND let normalize parse a
  64 MiB head that contains the blueprint. Goal met by the carrier fix alone.
- If it commits a `status:"failed"` runaway receipt, the disk is healthy and we
  can finally READ the truncated head (and the stderr tail) to see WHAT pi was
  spewing — a tool-call loop, an error-retry storm, or a legitimately enormous
  event stream. That diagnosis is the next move (prompt fix, budget change, or a
  pi flag), made WITH the head in hand, not on a guess.

Either way the lane stops burning 546s to commit nothing, and the operator stops
guessing. We did NOT touch the budget, the prompt, or any pi flag on speculation
— one variable changed, so the next run's signal is clean.

## The carrier discipline (tenth carrier wound, same shape)

Observability + boundedness are carrier properties, and they compound. #28 made
the lane say WHERE it died. #29 made it say WHY with the disk numbers, even when
the failing write was the one that would have recorded them. #30 stops the lane
from ever letting a misbehaving agent dictate how many bytes hit the disk in the
first place — the handshake the lane was missing. A resource that accumulates
must drain (#29); a resource that ARRIVES must be bounded at the door (#30). The
bucket has a hole in the bottom and now a valve on the inlet.
